{"id":"neet-2026-q005","pn":5,"y":"NEET 2026 · 21 Jun · Re-exam","tp":"Electromagnetic Waves","st":"EM wave in a dielectric medium","q":"An electromagnetic wave travelling in a lossless dielectric medium having a dielectric constant, εᵣ = 9, has the electric field, Eₓ = E₀ sin (kz – 2π × 10⁶t) Vm⁻¹ where E₀ is the amplitude and k is the wave vector. Among the following options, the incorrect choice is","o":["The wavelength of the electromagnetic wave inside the medium is 300 m","The magnetic field is given by the relation B_y = (B₀/v) sin(kz – 2π × 10⁶t) where v is the speed of the electromagnetic wave inside the medium","The direction of propagation of the electromagnetic wave is along +z","The speed of the electromagnetic wave inside the medium is 10⁸ ms⁻¹"],"c":0,"s":"Eₓ = E₀ sin(kz – 2π × 10⁶t) = E₀ sin(kz – ωt).\nSo the wave travels along +z.\nSpeed in the medium: vₘ = v₀/√εᵣ = c/√9 = (3 × 10⁸)/3 = 10⁸ m/s.\nFrequency f₀ = ω/2π = 10⁶ Hz, and it stays constant.\nWavelength in the medium: λₘ = vₘ/f₀ = 10⁸/(2π × 10⁶/2π) = 100 m.\nSo the wavelength inside the medium is 100 m, not 300 m, and option (1) is incorrect.\nNote: in option (2), B₀ should be replaced by E₀.\nNote: Option (2) as printed uses B₀ where E₀ is meant (publisher notes this); answer (1) is still the incorrect statement"},
{"id":"neet-2026-q018","pn":18,"y":"NEET 2026 · 21 Jun · Re-exam","tp":"Electromagnetic Waves","st":"EM spectrum applications","q":"The following table presents the part of the electromagnetic spectrum and their corresponding major applications.\nP. Microwave — I. For purifying the water\nQ. UV rays — II. For warming the food\nR. Gamma rays — III. For AM and FM communication systems\nS. Radio wave — IV. For treating the Cancer cells\nThe correct option is:","o":["P-I, Q-IV, R-II, S-III","P-II, Q-I, R-IV, S-III","P-II, Q-IV, R-III, S-I","P-I, Q-II, R-III, S-IV"],"c":1,"s":"Microwave is used for warming food.\nUV rays are used for purifying water.\nGamma rays are used for treating cancer cells.\nRadio waves are used for AM and FM communication systems.\nSo P-II, Q-I, R-IV, S-III."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q009","pn":9,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Electromagnetic Waves","st":"Relation between E and B in EM wave","q":"The electric field in a plane electromagnetic wave is given by\nE_z = 60 cos (5x + 1.5 × 10⁹ t) V/m.\nThen expression for the corresponding magnetic field is (here subscripts denote the direction of the field) :","o":["B_y = 2 × 10⁻⁷ cos (5x + 1.5 × 10⁹ t)T","B_x = 2 × 10⁻⁷ cos (5x + 1.5 × 10⁹ t)T","B_z = 60cos (5x + 1.5 × 10⁹ t)T","B_y = 60 sin (5x + 1.5 × 10⁹ t)T"],"c":0,"s":"In an electromagnetic wave, E and B are in the same phase and B₀ = E₀/c; their directions are perpendicular to each other.\n∴ B_y = (60/c) cos(5x + 1.5 × 10⁹ t) T\n= (60/(3 × 10⁸)) cos(5x + 1.5 × 10⁹ t) T\nB_y = 2 × 10⁻⁷ cos(5x + 1.5 × 10⁹ t) T"},
{"id":"neet-2025-q026","pn":26,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Electromagnetic Waves","st":"Displacement current","q":"A parallel plate capacitor made of circular plates is being charged such that the surface charge density on its plates is increasing at a constant rate with time. The magnetic field arising due to displacement current is:","o":["Zero at all places","Constant between the plates and zero outside the plates","Non-zero everywhere with maximum at the imaginary cylindrical surface connecting peripheries of the plates","Zero between the plates and non-zero outside"],"c":2,"s":"Let the surface charge density be σ = q/A.\nGiven dσ/dt = constant.\nSo d/dt (q/A) = constant, which gives i/A = constant (i is the current, I/A in the print).\nIt means the displacement current is constant.\nThis system acts like a cylindrical wire.\nThe graph of magnetic field B vs r is shown: B rises linearly with r inside, is maximum at the edge, then falls outside.","sfig":{"w":200,"h":155,"alt":"Graph of B against r: straight rise from the origin to a peak, then a falling curve that flattens towards the r-axis","d":[["l",20,137,20,7,"a"],["l",20,137,185,137,"a"],["t",30,18,"B"],["t",190,145,"r"],["p","M20 137 L100 60 C115 95 140 120 177 123","B"]]}}
